<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Managed stripping for WebGL runs at High, which removes any type the linker cannot see a
  reference to. Two things in this project are invisible to it.

  1. The Magic Words payload is deserialized reflectively:
     JsonUtility.FromJson<DialoguePayload> in
     Assets/Client/Source/Runtime/Adapters/Services/HttpDialogueService.cs.
     DialoguePayload, DialogueLineDto and AvatarDto are never constructed in code, and their
     fields are only ever read after JsonUtility has filled them, so High stripping is free to
     drop the fields — or the whole types. The failure mode is silent: no exception, no log,
     just an empty dialogue.
     [Preserve] is the usual answer and is not available here. Game.Simulation is compiled with
     noEngineReferences: true, so it cannot see UnityEngine.Scripting.PreserveAttribute, and the
     boundary that forbids that reference is the whole point of the assembly. A link.xml is the
     only way to protect those types without breaking it.

  2. DragonECS resolves component and system metadata through reflection in
     DebugUtils/TypeMeta.cs, over the attributes declared on our own component types.

  Both live in Game.Simulation, and that assembly holds nothing but game logic and plain
  structs — a few tens of KB of IL against a 33 MB wasm. Preserving it whole costs nothing
  measurable and covers every DTO and component added later, which naming the three types
  individually would not.
-->
<linker>
  <assembly fullname="Game.Simulation" preserve="all"/>
</linker>
